{"review":"approved","flags":[{"page":1,"note":"This deck is a scanned image; text was read from page images (OCR plus visual check). All 28 pages were reviewed."}],"recall":{"defs":[["Deep learning","Multi-layer neural networks that learn representations."],["Feature","Input attribute of an example."],["Label","Target to predict."],["Loss function","Measure of model badness to minimise."],["Learning algorithm","Procedure that finds loss-minimising parameters."],["Family of models","All functions an architecture can represent."]],"formulas":["\\mathcal D=\\{X,t\\},\\ X\\in\\mathbb R^{N\\times d}","\\theta^*=\\arg\\min_\\theta\\frac1N\\sum_i\\ell(f(x^{(i)};\\theta),t^{(i)})","\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta\\nabla_\\theta L(\\theta)","f(x)=f^{(L)}(\\cdots f^{(1)}(x))"],"takeaways":["DL = deep neural networks learning layered representations.","AI ⊃ ML ⊃ NN ⊃ DL.","Scale (data + compute) drives DL progress.","Data, model, loss, algorithm.","Supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement problems."],"confused":[["AI vs ML vs DL","Science vs approach vs technique."],["Loss vs metric","Optimised vs reported."],["Model vs family","Trained member vs all reachable functions."],["Multi-class vs multi-label","One label vs several."]],"minute":"Deep learning is machine learning with neural networks of many layers, each learning a more abstract representation of the data; it sits inside ML, which sits inside AI. It succeeded thanks to big unstructured data, cheap sensors, compute and storage, automatic feature learning, and the way larger networks keep improving with more data. Every deep learning problem has data (N examples with d features and labels), a model, an objective function where lower is better, and a learning algorithm, usually gradient descent. Problems are supervised (regression, classification, recommenders, sequence learning), unsupervised or reinforcement learning."},"flashcards":[["Deep learning","ML with multi-layer neural networks learning successive representatio